// A REAL PlaneGeometry, because the terrain is one.
//
// Everything else here can be scenery: the physics never reads a vertex. The
// ground does. Monaco's mountain and seabed are one PlaneGeometry whose every
// Y is written from the survey, and with a stub that carried no vertices at all
// the loop that writes them ran zero times — so a bug living IN THOSE VERTICES
// was invisible to the harness however hard it was measured. That is how the
// seabed came to lie exactly in the surface of the sea (#69) with every check
// passing. A test that reads the survey instead of the mesh is testing its own
// copy of the rule; this way the mesh itself can be measured.
